.SS Effect of minimum quality and length filtering (table 5)
.PP
Positions in reads are sorted in decreasing order, starting with the
largest read length (Len) and going down to half of it (rounded down,
with a minimum of 1), so the table has largest length - floor(largest
length / 2) + 1 rows.
The next four columns indicate the fraction of reads that would be
retained by the command \f[V]vsearch-fastq_filter(1)\f[R] if the reads
were truncated at length Len with the option \f[V]--fastq_trunclen\f[R],
or at the first position with a quality Q equal to or lesser than 5, 10,
15 or 20 with the option \f[V]--fastq_truncqual\f[R].
.IP "1." 3
Len: read length
.IP "2." 3
fraction of reads if truncating at Len or at first Q =< 5
.IP "3." 3
fraction of reads if truncating at Len or at first Q =< 10
.IP "4." 3
fraction of reads if truncating at Len or at first Q =< 15
.IP "5." 3
fraction of reads if truncating at Len or at first Q =< 20
.IP
